---
/**
 * What "current law" is on the runner, named once for both views.
 *
 * # Why the card exists
 *
 * Every figure the runner shows is a delta against the enacted act — `Outcome.delta` is "change
 * against current law" and the guarantee's default rule is `as-enacted`. That was nowhere on the
 * page, and a reader who arrived asking how the executive proposal compared with the House's had no
 * way to learn that neither is the origin, and neither is reachable from any lever.
 *
 * # Why this is a component and was two cards
 *
 * Both views carried a card headed "What the levers move away from", under the same anchor, and
 * they said different things (#562). `/scenario` named the act with its terms — signed 30 June
 * 2025, phase-in at 100%, base cost priced at FY2022 — and then the bill's earlier stages, which no
 * lever reaches. `/scenario/reach` named the act with its size — the total it pays, the districts,
 * the guarantee's share — and said nothing about the stages. A reader crossing the tabs met two
 * accounts of one baseline and could not tell whether they were one.
 *
 * They were one, so this is one text. What each view does *with* the baseline does differ — one
 * reports a difference against it, the other draws a trail from it — and that is the one paragraph
 * that varies, introduced by the view's own tab label so it reads as what it is.
 *
 * The same reasoning as `HeldFixed.astro` beside it: a caveat about the levers is the same on
 * every view, because every view has the levers.
 *
 * # Every figure is a difference against it, not every figure "below"
 *
 * The old `/scenario` text said "every figure below", from when the card sat above the levers.
 * #549 moved it under the results, where "below" pointed at nothing.
 *
 * # The worked scenarios
 *
 * The corpus's own scenarios start from the same act (#560). Said here because this is the card
 * that names the act, and because the two share a word: the class is a change argued in prose,
 * this runner is where a reader sets their own.
 */
import { NAMES, RUNNER_VIEWS, type RunnerView } from "../lib/nav.ts";
import { count, millions } from "../lib/format.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";
import type { Bundle } from "../lib/types.ts";
import SectionAnchor from "./SectionAnchor.astro";

interface Props {
  bundle: Bundle;
  /** Which view the card sits on, which decides its one view-specific paragraph. */
  view: RunnerView;
}

const { bundle, view } = Astro.props;
const s = bundle.statewide;
const tab = RUNNER_VIEWS.find((v) => v.key === view)!.label;

/*
 * The stages of the act this runner baselines on: drafts that are versions of it rather than
 * changes to it. Read off the feed by slug prefix so a stage added to the corpus appears without
 * this file being edited.
 */
const stages = bundle.drafts
  .filter((d) => d.slug.startsWith("hb-96-136-as-"))
  .map((d) => ({
    slug: d.slug,
    label: d.slug === "hb-96-136-as-introduced" ? "the executive proposal" : "the House-passed substitute",
  }));
---

<div class="card" id="baseline" data-part="baseline">
  <h2><SectionAnchor id="baseline" />What the levers move away from</h2>
  <p class="note">
    <strong>Current law here is <a href={routes.wikiNode("legislation", "hb-96-2025")}>Am. Sub.
    H.B. 96 as enacted</a></strong> — signed 30 June 2025, phase-in at 100% in FY{
      bundle.fiscal_year
    }, base cost priced at FY2022. It pays {millions(s.realized_aid_total).replace("+", "")} across{" "}
    {count(s.districts)} districts, {millions(s.guarantee_total).replace("+", "")} of it guarantee
    money. Both views of {NAMES.scenario.name} start from it, so the act itself is their zero rather
    than one of their scenarios.
  </p>
  {
    view === "changes" ? (
      <p class="note" data-view-specific>
        <strong>In <em>{tab}</em>,</strong> every figure is a difference against it: the tiles, the
        distribution and the most-affected table all read zero with the controls untouched.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p class="note" data-view-specific>
        <strong>In <em>{tab}</em>,</strong> every trail starts at the act's own position for the
        district, so with the controls untouched there is nothing to see moving — which is the
        correct answer, and the shape of the state is there either way.
      </p>
    )
  }
  {
    stages.length > 0 && (
      <p class="note">
        <strong>The bill's earlier stages are not lever positions.</strong> The corpus holds{" "}
        {/* The comma rides inside `set:html`: a `<Fragment />` followed by a comma on the next
            line renders the newline as a space, which puts a gap before the punctuation. */}
        <Fragment
          set:html={`${stages
            .map((d) => `<a href="${routes.wikiNode("draft-legislation", d.slug)}">${d.label}</a>`)
            .join(" and ")},`}
        />{" "}
        and neither can be dialed from here: a stage that suspends the formula and pays from a
        different base is a different formula, not a setting of this one. What each would have
        cost is on its own page, reported rather than run — and the three stages compared are on{" "}
        <a href={`${routes.wikiNode("legislation", "hb-96-2025")}#findings`}>the act's page</a>.
      </p>
    )
  }
  <p class="note">
    The corpus's <a href={routes.wikiClass("scenario")}>worked scenarios</a> start from the same
    act. Each argues one change in prose, with the figures it produced committed beside it; this
    runner is where you set your own.
  </p>
</div>
